Strings in C are just arrays of char ending with a null terminator '\0'.

```
char str[] = "hello";  // stored as {'h','e','l','l','o','\0'}
char *p = "hello";     // pointer to a string literal (read-only!)
```

Manual string length:
```
int i = 0;
while (str[i] != '\0')
    i++;
// i is now the length
```

Dynamic string on heap:
```
char *s = malloc(6 * sizeof(char)); // 5 chars + '\0'
if (!s) return 1;
s[0] = 'h'; s[1] = 'i'; s[2] = '\0';
free(s);
```

write() is a system call that writes raw bytes to a file descriptor.
fd 1 = stdout, fd 2 = stderr.

Signature:
```
#include <unistd.h>
ssize_t write(int fd, const void *buf, size_t count);
```

Print a single character:
```
char c = 'A';
write(1, &c, 1);
```

Print a string:
```
write(1, "hello\n", 6);
```

Print an integer (you have to convert first):
```
void put_nbr(int n)
{
    char c;
    if (n >= 10)
        put_nbr(n / 10);
    c = '0' + (n % 10);
    write(1, &c, 1);
}
```

Structs let you group related data together.

```
typedef struct s_point
{
    int x;
    int y;
} t_point;

t_point p;
p.x = 3;
p.y = 7;
```

With pointers (arrow notation):
```
t_point *ptr = &p;
printf("%d\n", ptr->x); // same as (*ptr).x
```

Malloc a struct:
```
t_point *p = malloc(sizeof(t_point));
if (!p) return 1;
p->x = 1;
p->y = 2;
free(p);
```

A linked list is a chain of nodes, each pointing to the next.

```
typedef struct s_list
{
    void            *content;
    struct s_list   *next;
} t_list;
```

Create a new node:
```
t_list *new_node(void *content)
{
    t_list *node = malloc(sizeof(t_list));
    if (!node) return NULL;
    node->content = content;
    node->next = NULL;
    return node;
}
```

Traverse:
```
t_list *current = head;
while (current)
{
    // do something with current->content
    current = current->next;
}
```

A function pointer stores the address of a function. This is how C fakes OOP and callbacks.

Syntax - pointer to a function taking int, returning int:
```
int (*fp)(int);
```

Assign and call:
```
int square(int x) { return x * x; }

int (*fp)(int) = square;
printf("%d\n", fp(5)); // 25
```

As a parameter (callback pattern):
```
void apply(int *arr, int len, int (*fn)(int))
{
    int i = 0;
    while (i < len)
    {
        arr[i] = fn(arr[i]);
        i++;
    }
}

apply(arr, 5, square); // squares every element
```

In a struct (simulated OOP):
```
typedef struct s_op
{
    char *name;
    int (*fn)(int, int);
} t_op;
```

Both static and global variables persist between function calls, but scope differs.

Global - visible across ALL files:
```
// globals.c
int g_counter = 0;  // 42 style: prefix g_

// other.c
extern int g_counter; // declare to use it
```

Static local - persists between calls, but only visible in THIS function:
```
void count_calls(void)
{
    static int n = 0; // initialised only once
    n++;
    printf("Called %d times\n", n);
}
```

Static global - visible only within THIS file (file-private):
```
// In myfile.c only:
static int s_secret = 42;
```

Why it matters at 42:
- Global state in GNL (get_next_line) must be static
- Avoid globals leaking between files in multi-file projects

Everything in Unix is a file descriptor (FD). 0=stdin, 1=stdout, 2=stderr.

pipe() - creates a unidirectional channel:
```
int fd[2];
pipe(fd); // fd[0] = read end, fd[1] = write end
```

Forking with a pipe (child writes, parent reads):
```
pipe(fd);
if (fork() == 0)
{
    close(fd[0]);           // child doesn't read
    write(fd[1], "hi", 2);
    close(fd[1]);
    exit(0);
}
close(fd[1]);               // parent doesn't write
read(fd[0], buf, sizeof(buf));
close(fd[0]);
```

dup2() - redirect one FD to another:
```
int file = open("out.txt", O_WRONLY | O_CREAT, 0644);
dup2(file, STDOUT_FILENO); // stdout now goes to out.txt
close(file);
printf("This goes to the file!\n");
```

```
int main(int argc, char **argv)
{
    int i = 1;
    while (i < argc)
    {
        ft_putstr(argv[i]);
        write(1, "\n", 1);
        i++;
    }
    return (0);
}
```
argv[0] is the program name, so real arguments start at index 1 and argc
counts the program itself. `argv[argc]` is NULL, so you can walk to NULL
instead of counting.

Most rank02 subjects end with a line like "if the number of arguments is
not 1, display a newline":
```
if (argc != 2)
    return (write(1, "\n", 1), 0);
```
Note `argc != 2` - one argument means argc is 2.

```
a & b     AND        a | b     OR
a ^ b     XOR        ~a        NOT
a << n    shift left   (x2 per shift)
a >> n    shift right  (/2 per shift)
```

Print the 8 bits of a byte, high bit first:
```
void print_bits(unsigned char octet)
{
    int i = 7;
    while (i >= 0)
    {
        write(1, ((octet >> i) & 1) ? "1" : "0", 1);
        i--;
    }
}
```

Swap the two halves of a byte:
```
unsigned char swap_bits(unsigned char octet)
{
    return ((octet >> 4) | (octet << 4));
}
```

Power of two, the classic trick:
```
int is_power_of_2(unsigned int n)
{
    return (n != 0 && (n & (n - 1)) == 0);
}
```
`n - 1` flips the lowest set bit and everything below it, so `n & (n - 1)`
clears that bit. If the result is 0, there was only one bit set.

Every backtracking problem is the same three steps in a loop.

```
void solve(int *board, int col, int n)
{
    int row;

    if (col == n)            // base case first, always
    {
        print_solution(board, n);
        return;
    }
    row = 0;
    while (row < n)
    {
        if (is_safe(board, col, row))
        {
            board[col] = row;           // choose
            solve(board, col + 1, n);   // explore
            board[col] = -1;            // un-choose (backtrack)
        }
        row++;
    }
}
```

Powerset is the same shape with a binary choice - take this element or
skip it:
```
void subsets(int *set, int n, int i, int *pick, int k)
{
    if (i == n)
        return (print_subset(pick, k));
    subsets(set, n, i + 1, pick, k);            // skip
    pick[k] = set[i];
    subsets(set, n, i + 1, pick, k + 1);        // take
}
```

fork() duplicates the process. It returns 0 in the child, the child's pid
in the parent, and -1 on failure.

```
pid_t pid = fork();
if (pid == -1)
    return (1);
if (pid == 0)
{
    execvp(argv[0], argv);
    exit(1);            // only reached if execvp FAILED
}
```
exec* replaces the process image, so nothing after it runs on success.
That `exit(1)` is not optional - without it a failed exec leaves you with
a duplicate of the parent running the parent's code.

Reap children so they do not become zombies:
```
int status;

waitpid(pid, &status, 0);
if (WIFEXITED(status))
    code = WEXITSTATUS(status);
```
Wait for all of them:
```
while (wait(NULL) != -1)
    ;
```

For a pipeline, the parent must close every pipe fd it is not using.
A reader only sees EOF once *every* copy of the write end is closed - and
fork duplicated them all.

```
#include <signal.h>

void handler(int sig)
{
    (void)sig;          // keep handlers tiny - just set a flag or return
}

signal(SIGALRM, handler);
alarm(5);               // deliver SIGALRM in 5 seconds
```
A handler that does nothing is still useful: delivering the signal
interrupts a blocking call, which is how you implement a timeout.

Work out how a child ended:
```
int status;

waitpid(pid, &status, 0);
if (WIFEXITED(status))
    printf("exited with %d\n", WEXITSTATUS(status));
else if (WIFSIGNALED(status))
    printf("killed by signal %d\n", WTERMSIG(status));
```

Write the grammar down first, then one function per rule.

```
//  expr   := term ('+' term)*
//  term   := factor ('*' factor)*
//  factor := digit | '(' expr ')'

int expr(char **s);

int factor(char **s)
{
    int v;

    if (**s == '(')
    {
        (*s)++;
        v = expr(s);
        if (**s != ')')
            return (unexpected(**s));
        (*s)++;
        return (v);
    }
    if (**s >= '0' && **s <= '9')
        return (*(*s)++ - '0');
    return (unexpected(**s));
}

int term(char **s)
{
    int v = factor(s);

    while (**s == '*')
    {
        (*s)++;
        v = v * factor(s);
    }
    return (v);
}
```

Pass the cursor as `char **` so every level advances the *same* position.
Precedence falls out of the nesting: expr calls term calls factor, so the
deepest rule binds tightest.

